    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d338">

                <pb facs="0557" n="455" sameAs="frus:frus1935v01/pg455"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A14/777a: Telegram.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Minister in Iran</hi><anchor n="7" xml:id="d338-f1"/> (<hi rendition="#italic">Hornibrook</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1935-06-08T16:00:00-04:00">June 8, 1935—4
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>16. In giving its advice and consent on June 6 to the ratification of the Arms
                    Traffic Convention of 1925, the Senate declined to agree to a reservation
                    regarding sovereignty over the Persian Gulf.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d338-f1" n="7" type="source" xml:id="d338-f1-content">
                <p>On March 22, 1935, the name “Iran” came into official use for the country known
                    up to that time as “Persia”; see Department of State, <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Press Releases</hi>, March 23, 1935, p. 181.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
